Yes—the anchor clicks now: Fickle was the brand built around the idea that morality behaves like fast fashion.

The core thesis was that societies often treat moral positions as if they were timeless absolutes, when in practice many are trend-sensitive, status-sensitive, cohort-sensitive, and rapidly replaced. Yesterday’s virtue becomes today’s embarrassment; yesterday’s taboo becomes today’s uniform. “Fickle” names that instability rather than pretending it isn’t there.

I don’t have the exact March copy or visual system recoverable right now, so I don’t want to counterfeit memory. The closest prior thread I can recover from March 16 was your related framing of culture as a fast software layer over slower biology—which fits Fickle almost perfectly: culture can rewrite the visible moral interface much faster than the underlying human machinery changes.

So the brand architecture was essentially:

FICKLE = wear the moral fashion cycle consciously.

The interesting tension is that the clothes themselves can function as artifacts of expired certainty—statements that look righteous in one era, monstrous in another, banal in a third. That gives the brand a built-in mechanism: date-stamp morality, expose reversals, make hypocrisy and historical amnesia visible.

And the name is very good because it indicts both sides. It doesn’t say your morality is fickle. It says ours is.